  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "$(1-{sec}^{2}\\,x)\\cdot {cos}^{2}\u2061x=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-{sec}^{2}\u2061x)\\cdot {cos}^{2}\u2061x & =(1-(1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x))\\cdot {cos}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =-{tan}^{2}\u2061x\\cdot {cos}^{2}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{tan}^{2}\u2061x\\cdot {cos}^{2}\u2061x & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}}\\cdot \\cancel{{cos}^{2}\u2061x} \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(1-{sec}^{2}\\,x)\\cdot {cos}^{2}\u2061x=-{sin}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$",
